#{[17592186193932 #edb/ref 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 4\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{2}{9}.$\n\n![](images/d3/d3c46b3452b6d5d22a30a9949800d7a69bdacf4135615627881c9dc69c0a5513.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $4$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $4.$ \n\nOur model has $2$ shaded pieces. So, the resulting model must have $2 \\times 4 = 8$ shaded pieces:\n\n![](images/b9/b99a75597e9e06cdd0c267b291ae0d671d670f7a3fc4b9af4555d9b217a0b4ed.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\\times 4 = \\dfrac{8}{9}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1065 8524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dbb3bf95-1b0b-52ea-b1ff-7a7a7c5d11f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193932 #edb/ref 1067 "A Faster Method for Multiplying Fractions by Whole Numbers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186486514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1045 "The Cartesian Coordinate System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186486467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485752 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "2ee8e36f-d6f2-535a-b7e8-f2094eda05e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485790 #edb/ref 1080 "(-3,-6)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218